{The most exciting game in the simultaneous display by the ex-world champion
Mikhail Tal was undoubtedly this one:} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5.
Nf3 cxd4 6. cxd4 f6 7. Bd3 fxe5 8. dxe5 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Nge7 10. O-O O-O 11. Bxh7+
Kxh7 12. Ng5+ Kg8 13. Qh5 Rf5 14. Qh7+ Kf8 15. f4 ({P N. Wallis is a
connoisseur of the French Defence, and his variation is one in which White is
tempted to sacrifice a piece for an attack which looks wonderful but is not
quite good enough.} 15. Qh8+ Ng8 {now would get White nowhere.}) 15... Bc5+ 16.
Kh1 Nxe5 $1 {Now we see the motive behind Black's last move: to leave the
white king's rook unprotected.} 17. Nc3 Ng4 18. h3 Nf2+ ({Black must have a
win here. Should he have played less aggressively in the sequel?} 18... Nf6 {
('N-B3')} 19. Qh8+ Nfg8 {('N-N1')} 20. g4 {had to be rejected.}) ({Stockfish
16:} 18... Ne3 $11) 19. Kh2 Bd7 20. Rae1 Qc7 {Threatening 21...Rxg5.} 21. g3
Qb6 22. Re2 Ne4 $2 ({This seems to us a losing move in a winning position. If
the goal of this knight's wanderings is only to be to give up a pawn for
nothing - in fact, less than nothing, as it powerfully develops Black's QB -
it might better have retired quietly to g6 on move 17. It is adequately
protected for the moment: make White work to eject it from its nuisance-post
in the middle of his pieces! We rather like the consolidating} 22... Ng8) ({
Development by} 22... Rc8 {could do no harm.}) 23. Ncxe4 dxe4 24. Bc3 Bd4 {
Forced, by the threat against Black s g-pawn. From now on, Black's game is on
the slide.} 25. Rd1 e5 26. fxe5 Bxc3 (26... Rxg5 {might have offered a hope, e.
g.} 27. Bxd4 ({or} 27. Qh8+ Kf7 $1) ({or} 27. Rf1+ Rf5 {and White is no longer
menacing both Black's bishops.}) 27... Qh6) 27. Qh8+ Ng8 28. Nh7+ Kf7 29. Rxd7+
Ne7 30. Rxe7+ Kxe7 31. Qxg7+ Rf7 32. Qg5+ Kd7 33. Nf6+ Ke6 ({He should surely
have tried} 33... Rxf6 {with some hopes even yet; though his king remains
terribly exposed and his queen's rook virtually non-existent.}) 34. Qg4+ Kxe5
35. Qf4+ Kd4 36. Qxe4+ Kc5 37. Qd5+ {Source: Birmingham Daily Post - Tuesday
21 January 1964} 1-0

